<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Viking Age</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 793–<a href="/years/1066">1066</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> Age is the period from the late 8th to the mid 11th century in which Scandinavians raided, traded and settled across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the North Atlantic. Its beginning is usually dated to the raid on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne">Lindisfarne</a> in 793 and its end to 1066. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> itself it saw the growth of trading towns, the first kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, and conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/viking-age.jpg" alt="Viking Age" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: historical museum · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bildsten_fr%C3%A5n_Smiss,_Gotland.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Many theories are put forward for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> expansion, and the will to explore likely did much to drive it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and Ireland were divided into warring kingdoms and vulnerable, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> were well defended. Overpopulation is one proposed cause, though some scholars reject it. Others point to growing urbanism and trade, as wealth moving along the routes of the expanding Islamic world drew Scandinavians to rich towns such as the wich towns of Anglo-Saxon England. Political centralisation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, especially in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, may have pushed people out, and advances in sailing, including larger sails, tacking and round-the-clock voyages, made distant targets possible.</p><p>The best-known beginning is the attack of 8 June 793 that destroyed the abbey on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne">Lindisfarne</a>, though Judith Jesch has argued that the age began between 700 and 750, since earlier contacts are likely. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> also records three Norwegian boats landing at Portland in 789.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Voyaging from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, Norse people settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-isles">British Isles</a>, Ireland, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faroe-islands">Faroe Islands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iceland">Iceland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a>, Normandy and the Baltic coast, and briefly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a>. In the east they travelled along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> routes, where they were known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangians">Varangians</a>. They raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iona">Iona</a> in 794 and monasteries in Gaelic Ireland from 795, and from 840 built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fortified-camp">fortified camps</a> called longphorts, the first at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> and Linn Duachaill. Their kingdoms and earldoms included the Kingdom of the Isles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danelaw">Danelaw</a>, Dublin, Normandy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>.</p><p>In 844 a fleet appeared at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagus">Tagus</a>, and in 959 an expedition under Björn Ironside and Hásteinn burned the mosque at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>. In the 11th century the Norse homelands were unified into larger kingdoms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Most of the age was pagan, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> was Christianised towards its later part, with early centres in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. Longships and the broader knarr, used for trade, gave the Norse a fearsome reputation. Important trading ports included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribe">Ribe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hedeby">Hedeby</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaupang">Kaupang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birka">Birka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>.</p><p>The Icelandic sagas are seen as characteristic works of the time, and Old Norse shaped later languages. More than a thousand Old Norse words entered Standard English, among them sky, skin, window, law and sister, and many place names in eastern and north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> are Danish.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Viking Age” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Slavic Pomeranians lived from farming, fishing, cattle and trade, and towns such as Wolin had been important Baltic markets in the <b>Viking Age</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Duchy of Pomerania · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Norway grew out of the many chiefdoms and petty kingdoms that lined the western Scandinavian coast in the <b>Viking Age</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>During the <b>Viking Age</b>, Swedish traders and warriors, often called Varangians or Rus, travelled the rivers of Russia to Byzantium and the Caliphate, and Birka on Lake Mälaren was a major trading town.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Norse religion was the Scandinavian branch of the religion of the Germanic peoples, which had developed over many centuries before the <b>Viking Age</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/norse-religion">Norse religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>By around 750 these changes had produced Old Norse, the language of the <b>Viking Age</b>.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-norse">Old Norse · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Viking Age</b> brought great change.</blockquote><a href="/culture/picts">Picts · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
